Closing is a move, at every depth: a child goes into its parent's c/, a root
goes into .pidly/c/. There is no marker file and no status field, so location
is the only thing that answers whether a ticket is done.

The semantics are settled and documented. What is missing is the command:
every close in this repository so far has been a hand-run mkdir plus mv,
including the ones that closed tickets about closing. Pairs with 11, which is
the same move in reverse, so do them together.

close writes nothing — it only moves — and must refuse while the ticket has
children outside its own c/. reopen moves back and cascades upward through
any closed ancestors, one move per level.

Done when both commands exist, check confirms nothing inside a c/ tree has
open children outside a c/, and the tests cover closing a leaf, closing a
parent with all children closed, refusing a parent with an open child, and a
reopen that relocates ancestors.

Both commands are moves and nothing else; neither writes to an info file,
because there is no status field to keep in step. close refuses while the
ticket has children sitting outside its own c/. reopen lifts every closed
level between the ticket and the surface, outermost first, one mv per level,
and removes each emptied c/.

Closed is "the parent directory is named c", not "the path contains a c/".
The second is also true of an open child of a closed parent, which is a
broken state rather than a finished ticket: check now reports it as "N:
closed, but child M is open", and reopen is its repair — which is why reopen
accepts a ticket that is already open when a closed ancestor holds it.

Done: implemented in bin/pidly, covered by tests/close.sh (26 cases,
including that closing writes no bytes to info and that the pair leaves
exactly what mkdir and mv leave by hand).
